{"purpose":"Validate whether Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is relevant and worth applying for serving EKA engineering knowledge to AI agents, and whether the EKA runtime database needs optimization for many concurrent connections — so the eka ecosystem (CLI, core, MCP server) handles growing AI-consumer traffic without architectural overreach.","content":"Theme under validation: RAG relevance + database optimization for many connections, scoped to the eka project (eka-cli, eka-core, eka-mcp). Two research questions, one theme. (1) RAG: EKA already exposes machine retrieval (ADR-015) and a Context Engine (ADR-021) that constructs deterministic context objects for AI agents; eka-mcp serves these over MCP. Does adding a RAG pipeline (embeddings, vector store, semantic retrieval over knowledge payloads) create real value over the existing deterministic retrieval, or is it overengineering? Where would RAG fit: retrieval over the knowledge base (690 objects / 940 payloads as of 2026-08-15, session time), note/comment evidence, cross-project knowledge? What are the costs: embedding infrastructure, index freshness vs immutable append-only knowledge, hallucination risk on canonical knowledge, operations burden. (2) Database: the runtime store is SQLite (eka.db) shared across projects; eka-mcp is a server that can receive many concurrent connections (many AI clients/tool calls). Does SQLite need optimization for many connections (WAL mode, connection pooling, read concurrency, single-writer model), or is the current access pattern already within SQLite's envelope? What is the expected connection profile of eka-mcp, and what evidence would justify a change (benchmarks, load tests)? Outcome target: a recommendation — adopt RAG (fully/partially) or keep deterministic retrieval; and for the database, adopt specific optimizations or document the non-issue with evidence. The theme must not contradict in-force knowledge: vis:vision, str:self-hosting, req:prd and derived requirements, accepted ADRs (notably ADR-015 machine retrieval, ADR-021 context engine, ADR-009 knowledge runtime).","investigationSummary":"Conflict/duplicate check (2026-08-15) against in-force knowledge: no existing vision, strategy, requirement, finding, ADR, scope, or plan covers RAG or database connection optimization in any registered project (eka, eka-view-fixture, nest-desacorp, qa-batch). Closest artifacts: eka/req:eka-mcp-production (approved — production readiness for the MCP server, where many-connections concerns live), eka/adr:015-machine-retrieval-interface (accepted — deterministic machine retrieval exists), eka/adr:021-context-engine (accepted — context construction for AI agents), eka/adr:009-knowledge-runtime-architecture (accepted — SQLite-backed runtime), eka/fnd:team-collaboration (approved — the only existing fnd in the eka namespace, unrelated topic). No contradiction found: the theme is additive, research-only, Discovery stratum. Constraint-check tooling note: 'eka context --depth engineering' currently fails on subjects whose neighborhood contains units with unqualified relationship targets (e.g. 'req:prd', 'str:self-hosting', 'adr:012-...' stored without the namespace prefix); the resolver rejects unqualified references by design (runtime/resolver.go, TestResolverRejectsUnqualifiedForms) while the store carries them — a read-path gap, now tracked as a bug work item (eka/bug:context-unqualified-refs, execution-state planned, published with this finding) plus a maintainer feedback draft. Workaround used: direct domain queries (eka get discovery/architecture) + per-line eka get. Architecture analysis (alex-architect, 2026-08-15): (a) RAG — embeddings rejected at current scale (690 objects / 940 payloads as of 2026-08-15 session time; scale unchanged since analysis): index freshness fights append-only immutability, vector similarity breaks the determinism contract of ADR-011/015/021 (result becomes a function of model version + index state), knowledge is binding so false semantic ranking is dangerous, cloud embedding APIs conflict with str:self-hosting, infra cost (vector store) is unjustified; the real retrieval gap is different — Knowledge.Search is exact-match only (no FTS/LIKE/partial) and the Context Engine (ADR-021) is not yet exposed as an MCP tool; gap-fillers without new infrastructure: SQLite FTS5 (built-in, deterministic BM25) + MCP context tool; RAG/vector becomes relevant above ~10^4-10^5 payloads or proven cross-project semantic recall, with mandatory guardrails (hybrid retrieval, pinned model/index version, provenance on results, canonical resolution never replaced). (b) DB — 'many connections' is a category error for the current architecture: eka-mcp is stdio one-session-per-process (server.go 'safe for one session at a time'), N clients fan out into N reader processes; WAL already on, busy_timeout(30000), read-only fast path (readers never take the write lock), SQLITE_BUSY retry+backoff tested; req:eka-mcp-production explicitly excludes HTTP/SSE transport and multi-session concurrency from scope; verdict: document the non-issue with evidence; optional single hardening: query_only pragma on the MCP open path; connection caps (SetMaxOpenConns) relevant only if a network transport arrives. Product analysis (althea-product-specialist, 2026-08-15): RAG premature as engine feature — semantic capability belongs on the far side of the MCP boundary (ADR-021 Decision 9), never inside the engine; FTS5 keyword search is the minimal valuable slice; explicit out of scope: embeddings/vector store, LLM generation/reranking, cloud embedding APIs (self-hosting conflict), cross-project semantic search; priority P2 low — additive, no conflict with in-force plans, concrete value = DB evidence for req:eka-mcp-production; do not derive requirements without user-pain evidence. Discussion (2026-08-15, user decision): graph-vs-RAG examined — EKA is graph-native (typed edges, stratum-aware Context Engine traversal) which is the actual agentic-AI advantage (traversal answers 'what binds X', 'what does X derive from' with full provenance, deterministically); GraphRAG-style synthesis is unnecessary because CKOs are already structured; vector DB examined and rejected now (embeddings chain: cloud = self-hosting conflict, local = ops burden; nondeterminism is fundamental regardless of store choice), recorded as a conditional option (revisit at scale threshold or proven semantic-recall use case; prefer sqlite-vec if adopted, as far-side adapter). Obsidian discussed as potential editor frontend (graph view, backlinks, MCP plugins) but excluded from this fnd by user decision. Discussion evidence: cmt:rag-db-optimization-implementation (role implementation, author Marij Mokoginta). Review: alex-qa PASS (conformance, state/changelog integrity, R10/R12); alex-reviewer PASS (technical correctness of the proposal content).","conclusion":"VALIDATED with recommendations. RAG (embeddings + vector store): NOT adopted now. Keep deterministic retrieval (ADR-015/021) as the serving contract for AI agents. Rationale: scale too small (690 objects / 940 payloads (2026-08-15) — vector indexing is pure overhead below ~10^4-10^5 payloads), the embedding chain fights the immutable append-only model: immutability keeps derived indexes (FTS5) trivially fresh, but for embeddings it forces full re-embedding of the archive on every model upgrade, putting the model version into the retrieval result, semantic ranking breaks the determinism/reproducibility contract (result becomes a function of embedding model version + index state), knowledge is binding (constraints, decisions) so false semantic ranking is a correctness risk, cloud embedding APIs contradict str:self-hosting, and ADR-021 already reserves semantic capability as a far-side adapter, never an engine feature. Vector DB: rejected now for the same reasons (store choice does not fix the embedding-model chain); recorded as a conditional option — revisit only at the scale threshold or with proven cross-project semantic-recall need, and if adopted, prefer sqlite-vec (local, self-hosted) behind the MCP boundary with hybrid retrieval, pinned model/index version, and provenance on every result. Graph: EKA's graph-native model (typed relationships, stratum-aware Context Engine traversal) is the actual agentic-AI advantage — traversal answers binding/derivation questions with exact provenance, deterministically; this is a strength to document, not a gap to fill with RAG. Database: NON-ISSUE at the current architecture. 'Many connections' does not occur: eka-mcp is stdio one-session-per-process, WAL + busy_timeout(30000) + read-only fast path + SQLITE_BUSY retry are in place and tested, and req:eka-mcp-production explicitly excludes HTTP/SSE transport and multi-session concurrency. Recommendation: document the concurrency model as evidence (no code change); one optional hardening — query_only pragma applied conditionally after the atCurrentSchema() fast-path check (a blanket query_only DSN would break schema migration on the MCP open path, since runtime.Open -\u003e Ensure -\u003e store.Open -\u003e migrate() writes on stale schema; the pragma must also come after journal_mode(WAL) in DSN order); connection caps only if a network transport is ever adopted. Gap-filler recommendation (partial adoption, zero new infrastructure): SQLite FTS5 with deterministic BM25 ranking over knowledge payloads (ADR-009 already reserves the content BLOB for FTS5; modernc.org/sqlite v1.45.0 ships FTS5 bindings; object_payloads is a rowid table so external-content FTS5 via content_rowid=rowid is feasible; note content is stored as BLOB so CAST(content AS TEXT) is needed at index time; column scope — index unit_json vs content — is an open decision, not both without consideration) exposed via eka get search and an MCP tool; and expose the Context Engine (ADR-021) as an MCP tool — both address the real retrieval gap (exact-match-only search, context not yet exposed) without embeddings. Session finding: read-path gap recorded — 'eka context --depth engineering' and 'eka get --upstream' fail on neighborhoods containing unqualified relationship targets; tracked as eka/bug:context-unqualified-refs (planned) + maintainer feedback. Open research items: (1) usage evidence — MCP tool-call logs to validate whether agents actually fail on identity-form discovery before committing to FTS5; (2) writer-concurrency load test (N parallel 'eka sync push', N=5/10/20) as empirical evidence for the DB non-issue; (3) transport evolution tracking — if eka-mcp adopts a network transport (streamable HTTP), re-evaluate connection caps and single-writer model; (4) FTS5 ranking contract — confirm BM25 determinism for identical content before adoption; (5) scale trigger monitoring — track payload count (e.g. via eka status in CI/agent) as the operational trigger for revisiting the vector threshold; (6) cross-project semantic-recall measurement — the revisit condition for vector search needs a defined measurement, not a vague use case. Out of scope by decision: Obsidian/editor integration (discussed, not part of this fnd), cloud embedding services (hard ban, self-hosting conflict). Next step per product priority: fnd stays research-only, P2 — do not derive requirements until user-pain evidence exists; DB evidence feeds req:eka-mcp-production."}